1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,956
Low blood sugar after meals - the shaking and cold sweats have a real cause.

2
00:00:04,276 --> 00:00:10,583
Here are 3 triggers behind reactive hypoglycemia.

3
00:00:10,903 --> 00:00:18,929
Feeling shaky, sweaty, or lightheaded two to four hours after eating is a classic sign of reactive hypoglycemia - a blood sugar crash that can happen even without diabetes.

4
00:00:19,249 --> 00:00:29,020
It starts when a meal spikes your blood sugar fast, so the pancreas releases too much insulin to bring it back down, and glucose ends up dropping below normal.

5
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:34,548
Your brain runs short on fuel, and adrenaline kicks in, which is what causes the shakiness and cold sweats.

6
00:00:34,868 --> 00:00:36,406
Three things tend to trigger it most.

7
00:00:36,726 --> 00:00:42,115
White rice, pasta, and bread, candy and sugary drinks, and for some people, a history of stomach surgery.

8
00:00:42,435 --> 00:00:49,996
First, white rice, white pasta, and white bread digest fast and spike blood sugar quickly, which can trigger an oversized insulin response.

9
00:00:50,316 --> 00:00:56,701
Second, candy and sugary drinks like soda or juice hit your bloodstream even faster than solid food does, making the crash sharper.

10
00:00:57,021 --> 00:01:04,918
And for some people, past gastric bypass or other stomach surgery changes how quickly sugar reaches the bloodstream, making reactive hypoglycemia more likely even years later.

11
00:01:05,238 --> 00:01:08,999
Pairing carbs with protein and healthy fat slows digestion and softens the spike.

12
00:01:09,319 --> 00:01:13,636
Eating smaller meals every three to four hours keeps blood sugar steadier throughout the day.
